##Motivation

Financial risk assessment includes portfolio management, derivative pricing,and regulatory capital requirements.
Value at Risk (VaR) is one of the risk measures that can determine the proposed distribution of asset returns.
Therefore, a proper distributional assumption can reduce minrepresentation of risk.


##Stylized Facts of Financial Returns

Empirical research has proved that asset returns depart from normality.
Mandelbrot (1963) and Fama (1965),The Variation of Certain Speculative Prices, The Journal of Business (1963), observed that price changes exhibit tails heavier than a Normal distribution. This observation holds for most liquid assets, including heavy tails, volatility clustering, and the absence of linear autocorrelation. 
the sample kurtosis of daily returns is typically well above the Normal benchmark of 3, which means a Gaussian model assigns too little probability to extreme events and understates tail risk. A student-t distribution is used to reduce this error.

##Student-t likelihood

The Student-t distribution is a natural alternative to the Normal because its degrees of freedom parameter ν controls tail thickness. 
As smaller ν gives heavier tails, allows the data to determine how heavy the tails are. 

##Why Bayesian Inference and MCMC
We use a Bayesian approach because it gives a full posterior distribution for μ, σ and ν, not just one point estimate.
This matters most for ν. It is needed many extreme days to tell moderately heavy tails from very heavy tails. With a prior, the estimate stays more stable,
and the posterior shows how uncertain we really are. We can also carry this uncertainty into the VaR later.

The posterior of a Student-t model has no closed form, so we cannot sample from it directly. 
We use a Metropolis-Hastings sampler (Metropolis et al., 1953, Hastings, 1970) and MCMC method that our group built from scratch.
For every stock we run 3 chains and check convergence with trace plots, R-hat and ESS. 

##Assets Under Study

We apply the framework to two contrasting assets.

VOO (Vanguard S&P 500 ETF) serves as the market benchmark. It is diversified, so it mainly reflects systematic market risk.
NVDA (NVIDIA Corporation) represents a high-growth technology stock. It is expected to show higher volatility and heavier tails because of concentrated, firm-specific and sector-specific risk.

Comparing the two allows us to analyse tail heaviness and volatility.
